  <div class="mod-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Module 01 &nbsp;·&nbsp; Dip</p>
    <h1>Everything starts with dip</h1>
    <p class="sub">Coherence, curvature and aberrancy are all built on one prior step: an estimate, at every
      sample in the volume, of which way the reflector is going. Get that estimate wrong and every attribute
      downstream inherits the error. This module shows you how dip is actually measured — by brute-force
      search — and lets you break it.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ============================ THE IDEA FIRST =========================== -->
  <div class="teach" style="padding-top:6px">
    <div class="tryit">
      <h3>What the computer is doing</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;font-size:14.5px">There is no formula that reads dip off a seismic volume. What
        the software does is <b>guess and check</b>. It proposes a dip, follows that slope across a few
        neighbouring traces, and asks how well the traces line up along it. Then it tries the next dip.
        Whichever candidate makes the traces agree best wins.</p>
      <p style="font-size:14.5px">"How well they line up" is measured by <b>semblance</b>: add the traces
        together, and compare the energy of the sum with the sum of the energies. If they are identical the
        answer is 1. If they are unrelated it falls toward 1/J for J traces.</p>
      <p style="font-size:14.5px;margin-bottom:0">Panel 2 shows that search happening at whichever point you
        click. The peak of that curve is the dip the volume will carry.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="takeaway">
      <h3>Why it is worth understanding</h3>
      <p style="margin-top:0;font-size:14.5px">Dip is not an attribute you usually look at. It is the
        <em>scaffolding</em>. Coherence is measured along dip; curvature is the derivative of the dip field;
        aberrancy is its second derivative. Each step differentiates what came before, and differentiation
        amplifies noise.</p>
      <p style="font-size:14.5px">So a dip volume that is a little noisy produces a curvature volume that is
        very noisy. If your curvature looks like static, the problem is usually not curvature — it is the dip
        it was built from.</p>
      <p style="font-size:14.5px;margin-bottom:0">There is also a fault in the model below. Watch what the
        dip estimate does when it crosses one, because that failure is precisely what the coherence
        attributes in module 03 are built to exploit.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

