  <!-- ============================== TEACHING ============================== -->
  <div class="teach">
    <div class="tryit">
      <h3>Try this — and what to watch for</h3>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Watch one search.</b> <em>Why bother: it is the whole method, and it takes ten seconds to
          understand.</em> Click somewhere on a dipping limb and look at panel 2.
          <span class="ans">One clear peak. The dip at its top is what gets written into the dip volume, and
            the parabola fitted through the three points around it is why the answer is not restricted to the
            candidates actually tried.</span></li>
        <li><b>Starve the search.</b> <em>Why bother: it is the most common way a dip volume comes out
          blocky.</em> Drop the candidate count to about 9 and look at panel 4.
          <span class="ans">The dip field goes stepped, because the search cannot land between candidates.
            The stat strip shows the dip resolution getting coarser as you do it. More candidates cost time
            and nothing else.</span></li>
        <li><b>Search too narrow a fan.</b> <em>Why bother: steep flanks are exactly where you most want
          the attribute to work.</em> Set the widest dip searched to ±1 and turn the fold amplitude up.
          <span class="ans">The steep limbs clip: the scan returns the edge of its fan because the true dip
            lies outside it. The dip field shows flat-topped bands where the structure is steepest. Real
            packages have this same parameter and it is easy to leave too small.</span></li>
        <li><b>Open the aperture.</b> <em>Why bother: this is the trade-off you are making every time you set
          an analysis window.</em> Push traces either side from 1 to 8, watching the fold crests.
          <span class="ans">A wide aperture is steadier in noise but it averages across changing dip, so the
            crests and troughs of the fold get rounded off. A narrow one tracks the structure faithfully and
            is jumpy. There is no setting that is right for both.</span></li>
        <li><b>Add noise.</b> <em>Why bother: it shows how far the errors propagate into everything
          downstream.</em> Take signal-to-noise to 3, then widen the aperture to compensate.
          <span class="ans">The estimate scatters, and the semblance at the winner falls. Widening the
            aperture recovers stability at the cost of detail. Remember that curvature differentiates this
            field twice.</span></li>
        <li><b>Look at the fault.</b> <em>Why bother: it is the bridge to the next three modules.</em> Turn
          the throw up and click right on the fault.
          <span class="ans">Semblance at the winning dip collapses, because no candidate dip can line up
            traces that have been offset against each other. The dip estimate there is unreliable — and that
            collapse in semblance is not a nuisance, it is the signal. Coherence attributes map exactly
            this.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
      <h3>If you remember nothing else</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Dip is estimated by search, not by formula.</b> A fan of candidate dips is tried and the most
          coherent one wins.</li>
        <li><b>Three parameters control it:</b> how many traces are used, how tall the window is, and how wide
          a fan is searched. All three are usually left at defaults.</li>
        <li><b>Aperture is a trade between steadiness and detail.</b> Wide is stable and blunt; narrow is
          sharp and jumpy.</li>
        <li><b>Everything downstream inherits the dip.</b> Curvature is its derivative and aberrancy the next
          one, so dip noise is amplified twice over.</li>
        <li><b>Where dip fails, coherence succeeds.</b> Low semblance at the best available dip means the
          traces cannot be aligned at all — which is what a fault looks like.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <details class="notes" id="notes">
    <summary>Method, assumptions, and where to read more</summary>
    <p>The section is a set of reflectors carrying a sinusoidal fold and a vertical fault, convolved with a
      zero-phase Ricker wavelet. Because the structure is analytic, the true dip is known exactly — it is the
      derivative of the fold — which is what panel 3 shows and what the error statistics are measured
      against. Reflectors are generated well beyond the top and base of the display so that a steep dip does
      not simply carry them out of the panel.</p>
    <p>Dip is estimated by the discrete semblance scan of Marfurt et al. (1998): for each candidate dip the
      neighbouring traces are gathered along that slope with linear interpolation, semblance is computed over
      the vertical window, and the candidate with the highest value is kept. A parabola through the winning
      point and its two neighbours refines the estimate so that it is not quantised to the candidate spacing.
      This is the estimator described in the literature and implemented in AASPI, written here in its plain
      brute-force form so that the search can be watched rather than assumed.</p>
    <p>Dip is computed on a decimated grid and interpolated for display. That is a performance choice for a
      browser, not part of the method — production implementations estimate dip at every sample. Everything
      here is two-dimensional: a single inline, so a single dip component. Crossline dip, dip magnitude and
      dip azimuth arrive in the next module, and the three-dimensional forms of everything that follows are
      built on both components.</p>
    <p>Left out: alternatives to the semblance scan, including the gradient structure tensor and complex-trace
      methods, which are more efficient and behave differently in noise; multi-window and principal-component
      dip filtering, which is how a dip volume is usually cleaned before use; and the fact that a real volume
      is migrated, so dip in the image is not dip in the earth wherever the velocity model is wrong.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Marfurt, K. J., R. L. Kirlin, S. L. Farmer, and M. S. Bahorich, 1998, 3-D seismic attributes using
        a semblance-based coherency algorithm: <em>Geophysics</em>, 63, 1150–1165.</li>
      <li>Marfurt, K. J., 2006, Robust estimates of 3D reflector dip and azimuth:
        <em>Geophysics</em>, 71, P29–P40.</li>
      <li>Chopra, S., and K. J. Marfurt, 2007, <em>Seismic attributes for prospect identification and
        reservoir characterization</em>: SEG Geophysical Developments Series, vol. 11.</li>
      <li>Barnes, A. E., 2000, Weighted average seismic attributes: <em>Geophysics</em>, 65, 275–285.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>

